 civilizations, active in Kingdom of Northumbria. Known in the fields of poets & playwrights.", "newValue": "Cædmon, active in the late 7th century (circa 658–680 CE) within the Kingdom of Northumbria, is traditionally recognized as the first Old English poet whose name is known. His significance stems from pioneering vernacular religious poetry, a crucial development in Anglo-Saxon literature that fused Germanic heroic tradition with Christian theological themes, profoundly influencing the nascent English literary identity and the Christianization of England. His story, recorded by Bede, positions him as a foundational figure in the transmission of Christian doctrine through accessible poetic forms.

According to Bede's *Historia Ecclesiastica Gentis Anglorum*, Cædmon was an illiterate cowherd attached to the double monastery of Streonæshalch (modern Whitby Abbey), overseen by Abbess Hild. He miraculously received the gift of poetic composition in a dream, leading him to compose 'Cædmon's Hymn,' a nine-line alliterative poem praising God the Creator. Upon awakening, he recounted his dream and recited the Hymn, prompting Abbess Hild to recognize his divine gift and encourage him to become a monk, translating sacred scripture into Old English verse. He subsequently composed numerous poems on biblical narratives, though only the Hymn survives in its original form.

